  "tools_enabled": false,
  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n3 3 3 3 3 3\n3 2 2 2 2 3\n3 2 0 0 2 3\n3 2 0 0 2 3\n3 2 2 2 2 3\n3 3 3 3 3 3\nOutput:\n0 0 0 0 0 0\n0 3 3 3 3 0\n0 3 2 2 3 0\n0 3 2 2 3 0\n0 3 3 3 3 0\n0 0 0 0 0 0\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n0 0 0 0 0 0\n0 7 7 7 7 0\n0 7 6 6 7 0\n0 7 6 6 7 0\n0 7 7 7 7 0\n0 0 0 0 0 0\nOutput:\n6 6 6 6 6 6\n6 0 0 0 0 6\n6 0 7 7 0 6\n6 0 7 7 0 6\n6 0 0 0 0 6\n6 6 6 6 6 6\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput:\n8 8 8 8 8 8 8 8\n8 0 0 0 0 0 0 8\n8 0 5 5 5 5 0 8\n8 0 5 8 8 5 0 8\n8 0 5 8 8 5 0 8\n8 0 5 5 5 5 0 8\n8 0 0 0 0 0 0 8\n8 8 8 8 8 8 8 8\nOutput:\n5 5 5 5 5 5 5 5\n5 8 8 8 8 8 8 5\n5 8 0 0 0 0 8 5\n5 8 0 5 5 0 8 5\n5 8 0 5 5 0 8 5\n5 8 0 0 0 0 8 5\n5 8 8 8 8 8 8 5\n5 5 5 5 5 5 5 5\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n9 9 9 9 9 9\n9 0 0 0 0 9\n9 0 1 1 0 9\n9 0 1 1 0 9\n9 0 0 0 0 9\n9 9 9 9 9 9\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    \"\"\"\n    Transform a grid consisting of nested rectangular frames.\n    The colors of the distinct layers are rotated outward by one position:\n    the outermost layer takes the color of the innermost distinct layer,\n    each other layer takes the color of the layer directly inside it.\n    All cells having the same original color are recolored with the same new color.\n    \"\"\"\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n    max_depth = min(rows, cols) // 2\n\n    # ---- determine the color of each layer (by depth) ----\n    layer_colors = []\n    for d in range(max_depth):\n        # the top\u2011left corner of each layer is always inside the layer\n        layer_colors.append(grid[d][d])\n\n    # ---- keep only the first occurrence of each color (distinct layers) ----\n    distinct_colors = []\n    for c in layer_colors:\n        if c not in distinct_colors:\n            distinct_colors.append(c)\n\n    # ---- rotate the distinct colors outward (right shift) ----\n    if distinct_colors:\n        rotated = [distinct_colors[-1]] + distinct_colors[:-1]\n    else:\n        rotated = []\n\n    # ---- mapping from original color to new color ----\n    color_map = dict(zip(distinct_colors, rotated))\n\n    # ---- build the output grid ----\n    out = [[0] * cols for _ in range(rows)]\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            depth = min(r, c, rows - 1 - r, cols - 1 - c)\n            orig = grid[r][c]\n            # every color in the input appears in the map\n            out[r][c] = color_map.get(orig, orig)\n\n    return out",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    \"\"\"\n    Transform a grid consisting of nested rectangular frames.\n    The colors of the distinct layers are rotated outward by one position:\n    the outermost layer takes the color of the innermost distinct layer,\n    each other layer takes the color of the layer directly inside it.\n    All cells having the same original color are recolored with the same new color.\n    \"\"\"\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n    max_depth = min(rows, cols) // 2\n\n    # ---- determine the color of each layer (by depth) ----\n    layer_colors = []\n    for d in range(max_depth):\n        # the top\u2011left corner of each layer is always inside the layer\n        layer_colors.append(grid[d][d])\n\n    # ---- keep only the first occurrence of each color (distinct layers) ----\n    distinct_colors = []\n    for c in layer_colors:\n        if c not in distinct_colors:\n            distinct_colors.append(c)\n\n    # ---- rotate the distinct colors outward (right shift) ----\n    if distinct_colors:\n        rotated = [distinct_colors[-1]] + distinct_colors[:-1]\n    else:\n        rotated = []\n\n    # ---- mapping from original color to new color ----\n    color_map = dict(zip(distinct_colors, rotated))\n\n    # ---- build the output grid ----\n    out = [[0] * cols for _ in range(rows)]\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            depth = min(r, c, rows - 1 - r, cols - 1 - c)\n            orig = grid[r][c]\n            # every color in the input appears in the map\n            out[r][c] = color_map.get(orig, orig)\n\n    return out",
